ofile":"Leon Ware (born February 16, 1940, Black Bottom, Detroit, Michigan, USA - died February 23, 2017, Marina del Rey, California, USA) was an American singer-songwriter, composer, pianist, keyboardist and record producer.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:21259","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":1288463,"name":"Lion Warfield"}],"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":["L Ware","L. Ware","L. Weir","L. Were","L.Ware","Lean Ware","Leon Wares","Leon Warfield","Leon Where?","Lion Warfield","Q. Ware","Wara","Ward","Ware","Ware, Leon","Wave","´Ware"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/21259-Leon-Ware","type":"artist","urls":["https://leonware.com/","https://myspace.com/leonware","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leon_Ware","https://www.imdb.com/name/nm1303679/","https://www.whosampled.com/Leon-Ware/","https://web.archive.org/web/20230128092927/http://www.leonware.com/"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/37731/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"37731","image":"https://i.discogs.com/RBZR85g9LlQ2nMh2sXO7JyTrkcl6h1XV9Z0oaq_xavY/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:337/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTM3NzMx/LTE2NDE3OTk3NzAt/ODI4NS5qcGVn.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/CBeK1_WSpCNN00yDnfvaJBifJ_Hx3cTWpRu3X_bkKCY/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTM3NzMx/LTE2NDE3OTk3NzAt/ODI4NS5qcGVn.jpeg","profile":"Chick Corea was an American jazz composer, arranger, keyboardist, percussionist, bandleader and recording artist of southern Italian descent, born June 12, 1941 in Chelsea, Boston, Massachusetts. This very prolific musician played anything from bebop, post bop, free jazz, Latin jazz and crossover to [a95546] and [a304968], as well as solo piano improvisations, including being one of those defining Jazz Rock and Fusion in the 1970s, both solo and in [a41893] and [a342265]. He acquired his nickname from an aunt who liked to address him as “Cheeky”.\r\n\r\nIn his youth, he marched as a lead soprano bugle soloist in a drum and bugle corps: [a9280684] of Chelsea, Massachusetts. Influenced by bebop, he started his professional career in the early 60s, and recorded his first album in 1966 (not released until 1968). After very well received trio Post Bop album [m115075] with drummer [a255556] and bass player [a128396], he began recording a series of groundbreaking studio and live albums with [a23755] 1968-70, before making 3 albums with Free Jazz and Fusion quartet Circle, as well as 2 solo piano improvisation albums in 1971-72. In 1971 he released the first Return to Forever album, and the band went from making Latin American acoustic and electronc music to a more Jazz Rock oriented approach during the decade. At the same time, Corea continued to explore Latin American and classical music, fusion and jazz, using mainly piano, electric piano and Minimoog. For the rest of his life, he kept on touring, recording and collaborating with numerous musicians, and was awarded 25 Emmys.\r\n\r\nChick Corea died from cancer on February 9, 2021 in Tampa, Florida. He was married to vocalist [a253240].","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:37731","relations":{"aliases":null,"groups":[{"active":true,"id":41893,"name":"Return To Forever"},{"active":true,"id":335789,"name":"Chick Corea & Friends"},{"active":true,"id":342265,"name":"Circle (5)"},{"active":false,"id":372754,"name":"Stan Getz Quartet"},{"active":true,"id":665991,"name":"The Blue Mitchell Quintet"},{"active":false,"id":736761,"name":"Rolf Kühn Jazzgroup"},{"active":true,"id":1020684,"name":"Chick Corea Akoustic Band"},{"active":true,"id":1069893,"name":"The Chick Corea Elektric Band"},{"active":true,"id":1076317,"name":"Chick Corea Elektric Band II"},{"active":true,"id":1186219,"name":"Chick Corea Quartet"},{"active":true,"id":1521041,"name":"The Chick Corea New Trio"},{"active":true,"id":1587212,"name":"Joe Farrell Quartet"},{"active":true,"id":2649884,"name":"Mongo Santamaria And His Afro-Latin Group"},{"active":tru